<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Choose the correct answer for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> If a matrix has 6 elements, then the possible orders it can have are:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1x6, 6x1, 2x3, 3x2</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 1x6, 6x1 only</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2x3, 3x2 only</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 1x3, 3x1, 2x2</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> If  A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\ 1 & 4 \\ \end{pmatrix}  and  I = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \\ \end{pmatrix} , then the matrix  A - 2I  is equal to:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 3 \\ 1 & 2 \\ \end{pmatrix}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\ 1 & 2 \\ \end{pmatrix}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 1 & 3 \\ \end{pmatrix}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \\ \end{pmatrix}</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Order of the product matrix of a matrix of order  3 &times; 2  and a matrix of order  2 &times; 4  is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 2x2</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 3x4</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2x4</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Not possible</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> For two matrices  A  and  B  to be conformable for addition, their orders must be:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Different</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Same</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Square matrices</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Diagonal matrices</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> If  \begin{pmatrix} x+y & 2 \\ 1 & x-y \\ \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 2 \\ 1 & 0 \\ \end{pmatrix} , then the values of  x  and  y  are:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) x = 2, y = 2</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) x = 3, y = 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) x = 1, y = 3</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) x = 4, y = 0</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with appropriate answers.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> A matrix in which the number of rows is equal to the number of columns is called a _______ matrix.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> If a matrix has all its elements equal to zero, it is called a zero matrix or _______ matrix.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section C: Assertion-Reason</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Read the assertion and reason carefully and choose the correct option.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Assertion (A): Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, i.e., AB \u2260 BA.\nReason (R): The number of columns in A must be equal to the number of rows in B for the product AB to be defined.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section D: Very Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Answer all questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Construct a  2 &times; 2  matrix  A = [a<sub>ij</sub>]  whose elements are given by  a<sub>ij</sub> = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">i + j</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2</span></span> .</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Find  x  and  y  if:  2 \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 4 \\ 5 & x \\ \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 1 & y \\ 0 & 1 \\ \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 9 \\ 10 & 5 \\ \end{pmatrix} </div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> If  A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 \\ 3 & 4 \\ \end{pmatrix}  and  B = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ -1 & 2 \\ \end{pmatrix} , find  A + 2B .</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section E: Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Answer all questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Find the product matrix  AB , where  A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\ 1 & -4 \\ \end{pmatrix}  and  B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\ 2 & 0 \\ \end{pmatrix} . Also verify whether  AB = BA .</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Given  M = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 8 & 3 \\ \end{pmatrix} , show that  M<sup>2</sup> - 4M - 5I = O , where  I  is the identity matrix of order  2 &times; 2  and  O  is the zero matrix of order  2 &times; 2 .</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;'>Section F: Long Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;'>Note: Answer the following question in detail.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Let  A = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\ 2 & 4 \\ \end{pmatrix} ,  B = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 2 \\ 5 & 3 \\ \end{pmatrix} , and  C = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ 1 & 4 \\ \end{pmatrix} .
(i) Find the matrix  X  such that  A + X = 2B - C .
(ii) Find the matrix product  (A + B)C  and verify your result by calculating  AC + BC .</div>
